  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nDry conditions will continue this afternoon into tonight as high\npressure slides across the Great Lakes, but clouds to thicken and\nlower this evening and especially overnight into Saturday morning.\nAs moisture increases and weak low pressure arrives, chances for \nshowers and sprinkles will increase on Saturday. Have kept PoPs in\nthe 20-60% range as coverage looks somewhat limited do to some \ndry air at the surface to overcome, especially as you work into \neastern WI. Highest chances will be in the afternoon or early\nevening. Amounts will be on the light side, under 0.10\". The \nchances linger into Saturday night, then dry conditions return on \nSunday.\n\nA slow moving, moisture-laden system arrives Monday afternoon/\nevening and sticks around into at least Thursday. Rain is likely\nMonday night into Tuesday (70-95%) with the initial surge of\nmoisture and forcing. Then chance/likely PoPs will continue into\nWednesday, then taper off on Thursday as the system finally exits\nto our east. Chances for exceeding 0.50 inch rain Monday Night \nthrough Tuesday are 70-90% with chances for exceeding 1.00 inch \nbetween 60-75% northwest of the Fox Valley, and 40-60% over east-\ncentral WI. Higher end projections take rainfall to 1.5 to 2.25 \ninches. Certainly looking like a soaking rain at times. Some \nelevated instability and impressive dynamics with the initial WAA \nsurge Monday night to include some thunder, especially across \ncentral and north-central WI. \n\nAfter a day with closer to normal temps, we trend back above\nnormal into the weekend, with highs on Halloween mainly in the 50s\nand in the low to mid 60s on Sunday through Wednesday. Temps cool\noff behind the large system at the end of the week.",
